t that I  always have a strong preference for  stylization hyper realism which part six  does phenomenally when it comes to its  characters but the location look it may  as well have just been a blank canvas  there were points where I felt why even  bother having a background at all and if  you're asking me well how do you propose  to make a prison story that isn't just a  flat jail wall then I would like to  forward you onto the impelled down Ark  of one piece because it can be done and  it can be done very very well  but to make up for the atmosphere stone  ocean instead delivers another fantastic  cast of characters helmed by one Jolene  Cujo who as many of you predicted and I  kind of love kind of because she has her  issues and she falls into the same Jojo  trap as every protagonist before her as  the story inside characters tend to ever  take her a lot but at the same time I  really enjoy Jolene's blunt to NASA  I will say that I was incredibly hyped  for her at the beginning though because  stone ocean initially presents us as a  classic prison revenge story where  everything seems set up for Jolene to  escape and ultimately take revenge on  Romeo kind of Kill Bill style and that  moment where she used her stand to  completely just murder the lawyer who  had misled her into this position was  incredibly gratifying so yes I was all  on board for a revenge story but then  things very suddenly change as it is  discovered that none of that matters and  from here on out this is going to be  mostly about Jolene saving her father  and I do think that's a bit of a shame  because it took the emphasis off this  being a quest for Jolene and put her in  more of a passive position needing to  save someone rather than do something  because she wanted to do it which you  know as much as I stated I wasn't a huge  fan of Jono and part five one of the  things I will give him is that he was  profoundly driven towards his own goal  whereas Jolene to me felt like someone  who has just swept up in something much  much larger than her now as for the  extended cast generally great all-around  and I want to first up give a special  mention to Foo Fighters because I for  one never thought that I would encounter  a colony of plankton that would form a  captivating character on any level much  less one that I would actually mourn the  death of but Foo Fighters was delightful  the plankton stand provided some  much-needed comic relief within the  group of primarily serious individuals  and just yet helped to break up the  monotony of prison life and also we also  did this successfully to some degree  with his ongoing quest to marry Jolene  at one stage even asking for Joe Torre's  permission in the midst of a climactic  battle but at the same time he was also  quite a terrifying character because I  always had the feeling that if you were  to get even slightly on his bad side  then you know you'd probably be swiftly  murdered but he actually becomes  surprisingly touching in the grand  scheme of things and the way his twist  of of Jolene manifests itself as a grand  sacrifice is creepily moving and moving  on I cannot and will never take a man  named weather report seriously which is  tricky because he ends up being quite a  key plot component but every time a  character said something like where's  weather report I couldn't help but  chuckle and yeah whatever I'm sure it  sounds much cooler in Japanese where's  re portal but yeah that's the thing well  they will say is that weather report is  a great name for a stand  not just any stand but one of the most  radically overpowered ones this entire  saga has presented but then again I  found myself saying that about almost  every new stand that was introduced in  stone ocean because some of these powers  is just downright sinister and I'm not  even talking about made in heaven yet  because even Jolene stern free  is pretty absurd but a lot of fun at the  same time and I think Eric you made  fantastic use of the basic concept of  strings but then you also have stuff  like Bohemian Rhapsody which is a stand  that can apparently affect the entire  world bringing fictional characters into  reality and tracking out the souls of  people and tying them to the fate of  said characters I think is insane  I mean it feels like you could almost  have an entire series about this you  know a world where fictional characters  are coming to life and the quest to find  the mystery behind everything but it's  all quite promptly dealt with in seven  tidy chapters I really enjoyed the  concept though and I actually think that  goes for almost every stand-in part six  even the more understated ones and in  fact especially some of the more  understated ones like kiss which is all  about creating duplicates with stickers  and playing with the idea of what you  can do when reforming objects after  removing a sticker with that said not  everything was a hit with me and I will  single out sky high as a stand which had  bored me for a lot of its story I mean  the idea of these mysterious and organic  roads was fascinating but the stand used  to control them and its user which is  kind of mad and it also came at a time  where we were really gunning towards a  conclusion and this became yet another  artificial obstacle to overcome oh oh  and before I move away from Stan's  completely I also want to name-drop  jailhouse lock as quite possibly my  favorite stand ever such a great and  terrifying concept but upon activation a  target can now only learn up to three  new pieces of information and reading  Jolene teal would that was quite  possibly one of the more captivating  experiences I've had with the entirety  of Georgia thus far and there are so so  many other stands that I could mention  and probably just can't remember at the  moment like the dragon one that's all  about feng shui so stands for pot six  were a huge success from my perspective  but let's start to get into the more  controversial stuff aka the last quarter  of stone ocean which goes absolutely  batshit insane and all of this is helped  by the ultimate series protagonist  Enrico Pucci who I will say I enjoyed a  fair bit in fact he's probably my second  favorite big bad guy behind your chicag  Akira at this stage anyway and I liked  his general theme of seeking ascension  and how that blended quite seamlessly  into the jury universe with dioz  influence and just on deer for a second  I think it's kind of crazy that the  brief moments he was featured in part  six actually provides me with a much  more satisfying character than how he  was betrayed in part three because  Stardust Crusaders deer was something of  a flat villain lots of fun but very flat  whereas here he seems capable of  actually being a character dare I say by  developing his relationship with Poochie  and even going so far as to admit a fear  of having could she betray him none of  this was the do I've experienced thus  far that quite like getting to see  another side of him and a lot of that  contributed to how I took to poochie's  motivation because this was a risky  story to tell and very very easily could  have felt like a villain doing villain  things for a dumb villain motive but  instead he does have a very interesting  goal which only becomes apparent once  it's in effect because everything before  that just sounds like babbling about  heaven and stuff it's a super intriguing  idea though basically going to the  effort to create a new universe where  every person has precognitive abilities  thus theoretically removing any sense of  worry and achieving a sense of bliss as  a result at least I think that's the  idea sort of like skipping all of the  stages of human emotion and landing  immediately upon acceptance every time  it is a very cool idea but I do think it  does come a bit out of absolutely  nowhere because part six like I said  plays out like everything else from Part  three onwards until all of a sudden we  are thrust into reshaping the entire  universe and that is a very quick and  very big escalation that I can see would  turn many people off because it may be  taken as a very jump the shark moment  and currently I'm very torn about how I  feel about it personally as a core idea  I love it and I'm happy that the series  built towards this sort of maddening  exploration of humanity but within the  greater context of part 6 I don't know  how well it flows narrative Li stacking  all of this weight within the last I  don't know what 10 15 chapters or so to  me it felt very heavy-handed and not  properly built up towards but I am very  much beginning to understand at this  late stage that that's just our key  sometimes he doesn't conform to  establish storytelling and as a result  some Jojo experiences can feel very  disjointed at times and there's actually  a vivid example of this I can recall  from much earlier in part 6 where Joe  leaned on us we and Foo Fighters  complete their business in the solitary  area in one chapter and then in the very  first page of the next chapter it's just  a hard cut to Jolene and Inasa we had a  doc with this damn yo-yo mast and which  nobody questions the existence of and it  just comes out of nowhere and it's  incredibly jarring it made me feel like  I'd skipped a chapter although there  were pages missing and what I was  reading and sadly enough that's not the  only time that happens in stone ocean  there were many times where I found  myself in a lesser natural progression  moving from chapter to chapter  which I guess is quirky in one way but  maybe annoying in another well there may  be bizarre is the word because there is  the one thing  that I will grant stone ocean 100% in  that it is the very first part to really  embody the namesake of the series this  felt like a bizarre adventure with all  sorts of crazy crap happening like the  protagonist being stuffed into a mouse  corpse a rain of frogs prison zombies  and even a 